// WQL — A KEY THE EMITTED KEY VECTOR CANNOT HOLD IS REFUSED, WITH ITS GROUND.
//
// ⚠ THIS FIXTURE'S SUBJECT CHANGED, AND THE REASON IS THAT ITS OLD SUBJECT NOW
// WORKS. It used to pin a `u64` sort key, refused because the key vector was
// typed with the class's REPRESENTATIVE (i64) and the `as i64` normalization
// REINTERPRETS above 2^63. The vector holds the key's own type now, so a `u64`
// key is collected as u64 and compared in u64's order — pinned working, with the
// values that used to invert, by wql_key_own_type_e2e. Refusing it would be a
// refusal of something the backend does correctly.
//
// What is left refused is the case the conversion table cannot see, because it is
// not about a conversion at all: a `String` key. Its equality is exact, its order
// is the byte order, and it IS its own emitted type — every antecedent about the
// VALUE passes. The vector still cannot hold it:
//
//   • the sort and the group scan read keys back with `Vec::get`, which refuses to
//     move a non-`Copy` element out of the vector's storage;
//   • the `str` view that would avoid the move borrows the row var, and the row
//     var's binding ends with the loop body while the key vector outlives it
//     (E0597, measured on this exact shape).
//
// ⚠ SO THE ADMISSION HAS TWO INDEPENDENT ANTECEDENTS AND THIS IS THE SECOND ONE.
// Deriving "may this be a key" from the conversion alone admitted `String` and
// then miscompiled it — `cannot move out of a value behind a reference (E0507)`
// plus three `cannot move a non-Copy element out of Vec` inside a generated
// chunk, measured at the parent commit. The remedy the message names is a `str`
// column, which the row stores as a view and which works today
// (wql_group_str_key_e2e, wql_join_str_key_e2e).
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use logos.mem.string;

struct Rec { pub id: i64, pub name: String }

pub deem sorted(src: &[Rec]) { from src s select s.id order by s.name }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
